A||||15||FieroLisa||Portable GPS/ Garmin eTrex||||1||
Z||000000||FieroLisa||12-09-2001||04:55 PM||||Hey guys!! I am thinking about how much I need a portable GPS for my car. Anyone who knows me, knows how directionally impaired I am.  But either way, my GPS only needs to have 2 fuctions for me to be happy... show me stuff like highways and roads of where I am, and also tell me my exact speed (the ol' Tracer doesn't have a working speedometer..not good for the 8 hour road trips) As for other criteria, just a readable screen. <BR>And so I got this gift certificate from Amazon.com and noticed that they had portable GPSs for sale. One I noticed was the Garmin eTrex, which is only 99.99 with no shipping cost. Sounds great to me, but I can't read thru all the technical garbage. Has anyone ever bought a fairly low end GPS and still had some decent success with it, or could someone fill me in, in simple terms about the Garmin eTrex? (cheapest one)<P>Thank ya's!!!<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/fierolisa.gif">       <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa" TARGET=_blank>FieroLisa's Website</A>*<A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ncfieros/" TARGET=_blank>NC Tarheels Fieros Site</A><P>AIM: FieroLisa||||reg||1
Z||000001||kslish||12-09-2001||05:38 PM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroLisa:<BR><B>my GPS only needs to have 2 fuctions for me to be happy... show me stuff like highways and roads of where I am, and also tell me my exact speed </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The eTrex doesn't have the capabilities to map highways and roads by itself....all it shows on the screen is the direction you are traveling, your current latitude and longitude and your aproximate speed.  It does have the capability to connect to a laptop computer with mapping software, but that doesn't exactly make it self contained.  It's capabilies by itself are pretty basic.<P>The Garmin eMap or eTrex Legend (about $200-$250) are the cheapest units I know of that can show your location on a map on its screen as far as I know of.  Even still, it may not what you are looking for.  Go down to you local Circuit City (or maybe Best Buy I think) and check them out there before ordering something mail order that you may not be happy with.  <P>Hope this helps     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Ken<BR><p>[This message has been edited by kslish (edited 12-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||12-09-2001||06:37 PM||||Lisa, youve seen mine. Its Garmin III. about all I use on it is the moving map screen. Theres lots more it will do but I never use it all, the manual is about as big as the bible. Get one like mine at Pep Boys for around $250 and the car cord is $20 more. Batteries only last about 8 hours. Get it, and Ill set it up for you and give a quick lesson how to use it. Remember though, they only show major roads like Interstates and US route numbers. The ones that show all the streets like intown, are lots higher, lots more complicated to work. Think the StreetPilot is the cheapest of those around $600+<P>If you need to save more from the $250, theres always some on Ebay. Just make sure you get Garmin GPS III or higher.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 12-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroLisa||12-09-2001||07:02 PM||||Roger, thanks, I was hoping you would reply, cause I liked yours but didn't know what kind it was. <P>P.S. The check went in the mail tonight  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||Archie||12-10-2001||08:19 AM||||I got the Garmin GPS V a couple of weeks ago at Best Buy.<P>I never realized how much i'd use it until I got it.<P>It has more detail that you can imagine.<P>Archie||||reg||14
Z||000005||CoolBlue87GT||12-10-2001||09:19 AM||||I've got the Garmin 12x handheld unit.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/delgar1.jpg">  <BR>I've used it in my other cars, and was surprised to see each one's speedometer was off a little bit. <P>Price :  Usually 100 - 130 dollars<P>Maps : No, but with the computer hookup, you can download information from Garmin's web site.<P>I've  used it for the trips to Williamsburg and Carlisle, and Daytona.<P>With the 'waypoints' (location on earth) the unit tells you how far away & est time to reach each waypoint.<P>Tell's you your speed, avg speed, miles traveled, Alt, has compass.<P>It tracks the past you've traveled, and has a 'TracBack' mode, it tells you how to get back to were you were.  <P>It's b/w display. Compared to the 800 - 1000 dollar units, it very basic in operation, those other units are alot better.<P>One thing I've done w/ mine, after the trip, I down loaded the information to the computer, then used a map from the Garmin site, and created a map showing the times & speed where I was every 5 min on the trip.<P>I printed it out, it was really neat. With that, I can now have an accurate map to follow, that shows me down to the min, where I am, and how long to the destination.<P>Good luck on deciding which unit you get. All of them are fun to use. <P>Takes the bordum of out the long drive - just remember to watch the road some of the time !!  lol<P><p>[This message has been edited by CoolBlue87GT (edited 12-10-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000006||Cliff Pennock||12-10-2001||10:27 AM||||As I understand it, handheld GPS systems are very inaccurate. They're off by about 20-100 yards. You can increase the accuracy by adding a special GPS car-antenna but even then it can still be off by anywhere between 5-100 yards. GPS-ready software use a trick called "snapping". It snaps your position to the nearest road. But even then it's still pretty useless for routing.<P>Car GPS systems use a lot of extra sensors to increase the accuracy. Bigger antenna's, compasses, gyros, speed sensors, etc.||||reg||1
Z||000007||CoolBlue87GT||12-10-2001||11:11 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cliff Pennock:<BR><B>As I understand it, handheld GPS systems are very inaccurate. They're off by about 20-100 yards.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Some might be that far off, mine indicates usually between 10 - 20 ft off.<P>I've noticed after the gulf war, the US made the GPS Satellites more accruate for the non military user.<P>Before then, mine showed an error of 40 - 50 feet.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Cliff Pennock||12-10-2001||11:19 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by CoolBlue87GT:<BR><B>I've noticed after the gulf war, the US made the GPS Satellites more accruate for the non military user.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Actually, they haven't made them more accurate, they just removed the offset. Before the Gulf war every sattelite was sending information that was off by a secret offset which was encoded and sent with the signal. Only the military was able to decode this offset. They removed this offset so now the sattelites are sending the correct information.<P>Still, handhelds are usually only able to receive the signal from 3-5 sattelites and you need the signal of at least 10 to accurately calculate your position. Triangulation alone is not enough due to the distance of the sattelites from the earth's surface.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Ken Wittlief||12-10-2001||11:59 AM||||good uses for GPS:<P>1. Take a portable unit with you when you have to leave you car in a big parking lot, like at the mall, or esp at an amusement park like WDW. when you park your car set a waypoint. When you want to go back to your car tell the GPS to GOTO that waypoint, and it will point you to your car - direction and distance, and you walk back to it.<P> 2. Amusement parks still - find out how fast you really are going down that big roller coaster hill - taking a GPS on a ride makes you officially a nerd!<P>3. downhill skiing - how fast was I going?! some units have a peak speed hold function.<P>4. really cool on an airplane - distance to destination, time till you arrive, and SPEED (Wow Im going 550mph!) - if they will let you use it on the plane and you are by a window.<P>5. ditto a train ride , though not as exciting.<P>6. even with a cheapo $99 unit, you can goto mapblast.com and get the lat/long for an address and punch the numbers in as a waypoint. then if you are driving to a place you have never been before it will give you direction and distance.||||reg||10
Z||000010||WampusCat||12-10-2001||12:08 PM||||That's proably true of the older handhelds, but there's an amazing dirrerence in the newer ones in both accuracy and the amount of time to get a position fix on startup.  My brother has an older Trimble and it takes several minutes from startup to 3D nav.  My Garmin III handheld is usually less than a minute and is always within about 20 yards of it's previous readings.  This is a blurb from Garmin's website: "Today's GPS receivers are extremely accurate, thanks to their parallel multi-channel design. GARMIN's 12 parallel channel receivers are quick to lock onto satellites when first turned on and they maintain strong locks, even in dense foliage or urban settings with tall buildings. Certain atmospheric factors and other sources of error can affect the accuracy of GPS receivers. GARMIN GPS receivers are accurate to within 15 meters on average."<P>  I remember when we first got "portable" GPS units in the U.S. Marines back in the late 80's. They were the size of a small suitcase and cost about $10,000.  All the satellites weren't up yet so we only had partial coverage.  It's amazing how far these things have come in a little over 10 years.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||rogergarrison||12-10-2001||01:01 PM||||She wants one with an actual roadmap that follows along with your car. The III shows that plus all the other stuff. Speed within .1 of mph, lad, long, backtrack, times till destin, average speed of trip, miles traveled, miles to go, marking certain points (like a fav restaurant along the way), direction, sunrise, sunset times, actual time to name some. The waypoint and tracking is mostly for using in boats and aircraft because you can go in a straight line point to point, most roads dont. Mine is very accurate, almost to point of showing which lane your in. In parts of Europe they now use GPS to actually land an airliner with no input from pilots. That is not legal in the US yet, or even legal for IFR flying yet either. FAA still requires pilots to use TACAN, ADF, VOR,s. Knowing Lisa, ID highly recommend the III,as all she'll need is the map display. All the other 'goodies' will just leave her baffled.||||reg||1
Z||000012||WampusCat||12-10-2001||01:15 PM||||GPS is FAA approved for IFR navigation and approaches now.  I don't know of any handhelds that are certified, but there are plenty of panel mounted units out there.  Check this baby out: <A HREF="http://www.garmin.com/products/gns530/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.garmin.com/products/gns530/</A>  <P>They don't make the III anymore.  They're now are up to V.  I think the III+ is still available.  It's basically a III that you can upload map data into.  The eTrex Legend  looks like a pretty cool map unit for about $250. <A HREF="http://www.garmin.com/products/etrexLegend/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.garmin.com/products/etrexLegend/index.html</A> <BR><p>[This message has been edited by WampusCat (edited 12-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||rogergarrison||12-10-2001||05:19 PM||||I didnt know that they approved panel mounted ones. Good to know. Ive always used my handheld. My old plane had an Apollo in panel when they first came out, it was a pain to use. Im strictly a VFR now, I figure if the weathers too bad to get there, then I prob cant do what I had planned. Didnt know the III was discontinued either. Im sure she wants one with its own map display, not one that has to connect to a laptop. Does the Etrex Legend have zoom in/out capabilites on map screen too? Looks like maybe thats the best now for her if it does. Didnt say in link. Oh and Lisa, it has a cool color case to match your car,  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><BR>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 12-10-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 12-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||WampusCat||12-10-2001||06:52 PM||||I have the Garmin III also and one of the things I really like about it is that you can make the display either horizontal or vertical and it has a neat dash mount.  The III+ is basically the same unit but with 1.44mb of mem and the ability to load more detailed regional maps in it with optional accesories from Garmin.  <P>III+...good: cool display and mounts, detachable antenna and cable.  bad: not WAAS capable, 100x160 display. $274<P>V...good: same as III+ with 19mb of mem, autorouting for shortest or fastest route + turn by turn directins, WAAS, 160x256 display(not bigger than III+ but more detailed.)  Bad: $410<P>eTrex Legend...Good: 8mb mem, WAAS, 160x288 display, $210. Bad: no detachable antenna, vertical only display.<P>eTrex Vista...Good: 24mb mem, WAAS, 160x288, electronic compass, altimeter/barometer.  Bad: same as Legend, $290.<P>The basic eTrex is a great deal at $109 if you don't need the built-in map and just want to use your own waypoints.  It will store 500 wp's and 10 tracks and it doesn't lose it's memory if the battery dies.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.advancedgps.com/default.asp" TARGET=_blank>http://www.advancedgps.com/default.asp</A>  <A HREF="http://www.gpscity.com/productsmobile.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.gpscity.com/productsmobile.html</A>  <A HREF="http://joe.mehaffey.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://joe.mehaffey.com/</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||watts||12-10-2001||06:53 PM||||Might not be TOTALLY what you want, but...<P>I use a super el-cheapo DeLorme Tripmate ($50us refurbished) with a custom made power adaptor to replace the 4 AA's, and a slight board modification (takes about 5-10 min's).  The mod lets you use the GPS as a normal NEMA device, so you're not forced to use the LAME software that comes with it.<P>I hook the above onto my old clunker 486-100 laptop ($200cdn), and run MS's Streets & Trips 2001.  I get worldwide coverage at a street level, with about 10-20ft accuracy.<P>Not exactly pocketable, but it still all fits nicely into my Targus carry case.  I take it on vacations when I'm really not into getting lost.<BR><P>------------------<BR>87/88 V6 5spd Notchback Hybrid<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/watts_sig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
